{"step_index":453,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-25T11:58:50Z","input_tokens":2049,"cache_read_tokens":218636,"output_tokens":1764,"thinking":"Initialization requires adding several DOM elements for visual components and animation control to the `initDOM()` function. These elements include toggle buttons, visual strips, gates, calculation stages, and negative reset visuals. The constructor also requires corresponding updates to accommodate these new DOM elements.\n\n\nAnimations are enabled by default, and a toggle button controls their state. This toggle updates text and CSS classes to reflect the animation status and disables animations by adding a class to the document body. Visualization of variable boxes will be implemented using a grid of cards, including display of previous values and negative warnings. Calculation stages will visually represent assignment and augmented assignment operations with animated transitions.\n\n\nThe negative sum visual will display when a variable 'c' becomes negative, signaling that the current continuous subarray sum is counterproductive. This visual aids in understanding the strategy of resetting the sum to zero when negative, to avoid diminishing future subarray sums. Condition comparison visuals will also display, highlighting the evaluated condition's operands, operator, result, and associated branch direction.\n\n\nNow focusing on the conditional gate, it highlights the comparison between the current sum and the best sum found so far. The visual feedback explicitly labels the executed branch (update or skip) based on the condition's outcome. Loop visualization is next, which will track the progress of the algorithm through the array with a pointer. Finally, a loop return banner shows the iteration status, element value, and how the loop moves to the next iteration.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer\\app_build\\app.js","EndLine":645,"StartLine":615,"toolAction":"Viewing renderArea1Code in app.js","toolSummary":"View app.js lines 615-645"}}]}
